    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">How the VA Loan Actually Works and What Makes It Different From Every Other Mortgage</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you've served in the US military and haven't used your VA loan benefit yet, or if you used it once years ago and aren't sure how it works now, this article is written for you. The VA loan is one of the most valuable financial benefits available to service members and veterans, and it is consistently underused because most people who are entitled to it don't fully understand what it actually offers.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What the VA Loan Does That No Other Mortgage Does</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Most mortgage products require a down payment, charge private mortgage insurance if your equity is below a certain threshold, and qualify you based on a debt-to-income ratio that often leaves little room for the realities of military pay and service-related income. The VA loan eliminates all three of those constraints simultaneously. Eligible borrowers can purchase a home with no down payment, pay no private mortgage insurance regardless of how much equity they hold, and qualify under guidelines that are specifically designed to account for military compensation structures. No other widely available mortgage program offers all three of those features together.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Value Most Veterans Don't Fully Appreciate</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Private mortgage insurance on a conventional loan with a low down payment typically costs between 0.5% and 1.5% of the loan amount annually. On a home in the mid-range of most markets, that's several thousand dollars per year added to the cost of carrying the mortgage, for years, until enough equity is built to remove it. The VA loan eliminates that cost for the life of the loan regardless of the down payment amount. Over a typical loan term, that elimination represents a significant financial advantage.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The no-down-payment feature compounds that advantage further. A conventional buyer putting 20% down has deployed a substantial amount of capital to avoid mortgage insurance. A VA buyer achieves the same result without deploying any of it, keeping that capital available for other uses.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How VA Loan Eligibility Actually Works</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">VA loan eligibility is based on your service history and is established through a Certificate of Eligibility, or COE, which the VA issues to confirm that you meet the service requirements for the benefit. Most lenders can pull your COE directly through the VA's automated system, meaning you don't need to obtain it separately before you apply.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Service requirements vary depending on whether you served on active duty, in the National Guard or Reserves, or as a surviving spouse. Active duty veterans generally need 90 days of service during wartime or 181 days during peacetime. National Guard and Reserve members have different thresholds depending on when and how they served. A lender experienced with VA loans can confirm your eligibility quickly at the start of the application process.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The property must be your primary residence. VA loans are not available for investment properties or vacation homes, and the borrower must certify their intent to occupy the home within a reasonable time after closing.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Confirm your eligibility before you do anything else.</strong> If you've served, there's a strong likelihood you qualify. A VA-experienced lender can pull your Certificate of Eligibility in minutes and confirm your entitlement amount.</li>
      <li><strong>Get pre-approved through a lender who regularly closes VA loans.</strong> VA loan guidelines differ from conventional guidelines in meaningful ways. A lender with VA experience will structure your application correctly from the start.</li>
      <li><strong>Understand the VA funding fee before you close.</strong> Most VA borrowers pay a funding fee at closing, which helps sustain the program. The fee varies based on your service category, down payment amount, and whether you've used the benefit before. Veterans with a service-connected disability rating of 10% or higher are exempt from the funding fee.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not assume a no-down-payment purchase is always the right strategy.</strong> The VA loan allows zero down, but putting some amount down reduces your loan balance, your monthly payment, and your total interest paid. Run both scenarios before you decide how much to put down.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep your Certificate of Eligibility and DD-214 accessible throughout the process.</strong> These documents will be requested during underwriting and having them ready prevents delays.</li>
    </ol>

